==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|pleden}}, {{m|enm|plaiden}}, from {{der|en|fro|plaider||to plead, offer a plea}}, from {{m|fro|plait}}, from {{der|en|ML.|placitum||a decree, sentence, suit, plea, etc.", in Classical {{der|en|la|-}}, "an opinion, determination, prescription, order; literally, that which is pleasing, pleasure}}, neuter of {{m|la|placitus}}, past participle of {{m|la|placeō||to please}}. Cognate with {{cog|es|pleitear||to litigate, take to court}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/pliːd/|q1=infinitive, present tense}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-plead.ogg|a=US}}
** {{rhymes|en|iːd|s=1}}
* {{IPA|en|/plɛd/|q1=past tense, participle}}
** {{rhymes|en|ɛd|s=1}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|past2=pled|past2_qual=chiefly North America, Scotland|past3=plead}}

# {{lb|en|ambitransitive|copulative}} To present (an argument or a [[plea]]), especially in a legal case.
#: {{ux|en|The defendant has decided to '''plead''' not guilty.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Job|16|21|passage=O that one might '''plead''' for a man with God, as a man '''pleadeth''' for his neighbour!}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2023 October 18|title=Network News: Carmont: NR pays nearly £1m in out-of-court settlements|journal=RAIL|issue=994|page=15|text=At the High Court in Aberdeen in September, NR '''pleaded''' guilty to a series of failings, including failing to tell the driver that it was unsafe to drive the train at the 75mph line speed.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To beg, beseech, or implore, especially emotionally.
#: {{syn|en|appeal|ask|beg|request}}
#: {{ux|en|He '''pleaded''' with me not to leave the house.}}
#: {{ux|en|He was '''pleading''' for mercy.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To offer by way of [[excuse]].
#: {{syn|en|allege|claim|maintain}}
#: {{ux|en|Not wishing to attend the banquet, I '''pleaded''' illness.}}
#: {{ux|en|It is no defence to '''plead''' that you were only obeying orders.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[discuss]] by [[argument]]s.

====Usage notes====
* The irregular past form is always pronounced {{IPAchar|/plɛd/}}, but may be spelt {{m|en||pled}} (analogous to {{m|en|led}}) or less often {{m|en||plead}} (analogous to {{m|en|read}}).
* A majority of North American speakers naturally use the irregular form. The form {{m|en||pleaded}} is perceived as “wrong” by some speakers, while others perceive it as formal. Both forms are common in Scotland. In most other areas only {{m|en||pleaded}} is usual.

